# Rendered by Terraform for the Bee hosts on Vultr. Two ways to use it:
#
#   ssh -F terraform/rendered/vultr/ssh_config <alias>
#
# or, once, in ~/.ssh/config — which is the one that matters, because
# streaming-infra-manager's deploy/deploy.sh calls bare `ssh <target>` and never `ssh -F`:
#
#   Include ${self_path}
#
# Put that line above your own Host blocks: at top level it is global, and OpenSSH silently
# ignores an Include whose file does not exist, so the line is safe to add before this file has
# ever been rendered.
#
# The aliases MUST stay dotless. swarm-hls-stream's host_from_target only resolves a target
# through `ssh -G` when the name has no dot in it; a dotted alias is taken for a literal
# hostname and the lookup breaks.
#
# UNLIKE the GCP root's ssh_config, these are direct TCP connections to a public address, so the
# host key is the only identity there is and pinning it is not optional. StrictHostKeyChecking
# accept-new records the key on first connect and refuses a CHANGED one; the known_hosts file is
# per-root and scoped to these hosts, so after a deliberate rebuild the fix is deleting that one
# file — never `ssh-keygen -R` against ~/.ssh/known_hosts, and never a global StrictHostKeyChecking
# no that would also weaken every other target you ssh to.
%{ for host in hosts ~}
Host ${host.alias}
  HostName ${host.ip}
  User ${user}
  IdentityFile ${identity_file}
  StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
  UserKnownHostsFile ${known_hosts}

%{ endfor ~}
